WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.300
INTRO:
Over the past several years, Apple has dominated

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:05.550
the top of the fortune 500.

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:10.110
From being the first company to reach a $1
trillion valuation to being the first company

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:12.719
to reach a $3 trillion valuation.

00:00:12.719 --> 00:00:17.090
Much of this success is often credited to
Steve Jobs which makes sense given that he

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:21.520
was the visionary who gave Apple some of its
most iconic ideas and attributes.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:27.710
With that being said though, Tim Cook has
now been the CEO of Apple for over 10 years,

00:00:27.710 --> 00:00:32.649
and during that time period, he’s had some
of the best execution and least drama of any

00:00:32.649 --> 00:00:34.420
silicon valley executive.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:38.510
From overseeing Apple foray’s into services
to leading new product launches such as the

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:43.629
Apple Watch, Airpods, the M1 chip, and potentially
soon the Apple Car and Apple Glasses.

00:00:43.629 --> 00:00:49.020
There’s no doubt that Apple wouldn’t be
what they are today without Tim Cook, but

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:52.379
it seems like Cook’s journey at the top
is nearly coming to an end.

00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:56.569
Rumors suggest that Cook is looking into a
succession plan and that he’ll retire after

00:00:56.569 --> 00:01:00.160
he oversees the launch of one more major product
category.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.739
Considering this, I thought it would be a
great time to look back at Cook’s past and

00:01:03.739 --> 00:01:07.520
analyze how he became the CEO of Apple.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:13.399
PRE APPLE:
Taking a look back, Tim’s story dates back

00:01:13.399 --> 00:01:18.100
to November 1, 1960 to a small town in Alabama
called Mobile.

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:22.600
He grew up with two brothers named Gerald
and Michael in a pretty average American household.

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:28.020
Tim’s father, Donald, worked at a shipyard
while his mother, Geraldine, worked at a pharmacy.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.610
It seems that his parents were strong Christians
as Tim would be baptized as a kid.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:37.259
Anyway, Tim would end up attending Robertsdale
High School where he quickly became a star

00:01:37.259 --> 00:01:38.259
student.

00:01:38.259 --> 00:01:42.609
He graduated rank 2 in his class and he would
enroll in a local state college called Auburn

00:01:42.609 --> 00:01:43.609
University.

00:01:43.609 --> 00:01:47.890
While Tim always wanted to work at a computer
company, it doesn’t seem like he wanted

00:01:47.890 --> 00:01:51.200
to be a programmer or a tech person per se.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.030
It seems like he was always more interested
in the operations side of companies, and appropriately,

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:58.100
he majored in industrial engineering.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:02.590
One of his first jobs after graduation was
at IBM where he mainly worked on their supply

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:03.590
chain.

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:08.950
While working at IBM, Tim also secured an
MBA from Duke University in 1988.

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:12.800
This allowed him to climb the corporate ladder
even quicker, and Tim soon became the North

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.519
American fulfillment director at IBM.

00:02:15.519 --> 00:02:20.709
Despite this rapid progress though, Tim decided
to leave IBM after 12 years at the company.

00:02:20.709 --> 00:02:26.620
In 1994, Tim was hired as the COO of a small
computer retailer named Intelligent Electronics.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:31.239
He worked here for the next 3 years before
jumping over to Compaq Computer Corporation

00:02:31.239 --> 00:02:33.020
as vice president.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:36.870
Around this time, Apple was doing extremely
bad.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:40.220
Revenue was diving off a cliff and the company
was on the verge of bankruptcy.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:44.879
Meanwhile, Steve Jobs had been away from the
company for over a decade.

00:02:44.879 --> 00:02:50.110
It wasn’t until Apple agreed to acquire
NEXT Software that Steve would return to Apple.

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:54.680
Originally, Steve was just supposed to be
a temporary CEO, but it quickly became clear

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:59.129
that no one else was better for the role especially
given the circumstances.

00:02:59.129 --> 00:03:03.489
As Steve assembled a new executive team to
hopefully revive Apple, one of the people

00:03:03.489 --> 00:03:06.830
he invited to join the company was of course
Tim Cook.

00:03:06.830 --> 00:03:11.860
At first, Tim wasn’t sure if he was actually
going to join Apple, but one thing he was

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:15.400
sure of was that he would at least meet with
Steve Jobs.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:19.720
Like many of us, Tim himself was a massive
fan of Steve who he thought was a creative

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:20.720
genius.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:24.890
But, at the meeting, Steve talked to Tim about
his vision for what would eventually become

00:03:24.890 --> 00:03:25.950
the iMac.

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:30.459
And after hearing Steve describe his vision,
Tim knew that he was gonna do it.

00:03:30.459 --> 00:03:35.250
When Tim shared this news with his friends
and family, they all thought that he was crazy.

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:39.170
To give up an executive role at an established
computer company in order to join one that

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:42.390
was nearly extinct was completely irrational.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:45.849
Tim admits that there’s no way to logically
justify his actions.

00:03:45.849 --> 00:03:50.489
But, with that being said, Tim also knew that
working with Steve was a once in a lifetime

00:03:50.489 --> 00:03:51.489
opportunity.

00:03:51.489 --> 00:03:55.540
So, Tim listened to his gut and joined Apple
in 1998.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:04:01.290
SUPPLY CHAIN WIZARD:
Despite joining a company that’s known for

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:06.590
their beautiful products and UI, Tim never
really worked on either of these aspects.

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:10.700
He knew that his expertise was within the
supply chain, and that’s where he focused

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:13.110
his efforts as senior vice president of worldwide
operations.

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:17.329
Tim’s first order of business was purging
Apple’s existing supply chain to reduce

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:18.440
complexity.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:23.470
Tim slashed the number of supply chain vendors
from over a 100 down to just 24.

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:27.750
He also reduced the number of warehouses by
half, and he started shifting Apple towards

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:29.390
contract manufacturing.

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:33.000
Contract manufacturing is basically when you
hire third party companies to handle various

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.770
parts of the production and assembly process
like TSMC and Foxconn.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:41.970
These companies also work with dozens of other
tech companies which allows them to reach

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:44.190
extraordinary economies of scale.

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:48.630
Aside from cleaning up the supply chain, Tim
spent a lot of his effort on inventory management.

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:54.510
One of the crown jewels of Apple’s supply
chain is just in time or JIT manufacturing,

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:57.120
and its roots can be traced back to Tim.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:01.310
JIT manufacturing is the idea of eliminating
inventory.

00:05:01.310 --> 00:05:06.520
In an ideal JIT implementation, stores would
carry absolutely no inventory.

00:05:06.520 --> 00:05:10.660
This isn’t to say that you can’t go to
the store and buy the given product.

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:14.940
If you were to go to the store, the product
should ideally arrive at the store seconds

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:16.600
before you buy it.

00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:21.949
Obviously, this isn’t very realistic, but
Tim was able to leverage JIT to reduce Apple’s

00:05:21.949 --> 00:05:26.460
inventory from weeks of inventory to less
than 5 days worth of inventory.

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:30.830
This might not sound like a big deal, but
if you’ve ever run a business, and especially

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:36.259
a small business, you know that storing and
managing inventory is extremely expensive.

00:05:36.259 --> 00:05:37.750
Take the iPhone for example.

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:42.300
Apple stopped releasing unit sales numbers
for the iPhone years ago, but let’s just

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:44.450
assume that it's 100 million units per year.

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:45.450
And let’s also assume that each iPhone costs
Apple $500 from their end.

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:51.100
If Apple were to store 5 weeks worth of inventory,
they would have to sink $5 billion into inventory.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:55.139
If they only had to store 5 days worth of
inventory though, they would only have to

00:05:55.139 --> 00:05:58.240
sink $685 million into inventory.

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:02.810
Again, this doesn’t really matter that much
today as they have plenty of cash.

00:06:02.810 --> 00:06:07.419
But, back in the early 2000s, this was crucial
to cutting costs.

00:06:07.419 --> 00:06:12.710
And with the help of contract and JIT manufacturing
which reduced costs and the help of the iMac

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:17.480
and the iPod which increased revenue, Apple
was finally able to regain profitability.

00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:22.330
This allowed Steve to focus on the iPhone
which was of course a massive success, but

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:23.860
all was not good.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:27.650
Steve was diagnosed with pancreatic cancer
which made it rather difficult for him to

00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:29.800
juggle Apple and his personal health.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:34.220
At one point, Steve actually needed a liver
transplant, and some sources suggest that

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:38.750
Tim offered to donate a portion of his liver,
but Steve declined the offer.

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:43.250
While Steve declined Tim’s offer, as Steve’s
health continued to decline, he delegated

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:48.840
more and more tasks to Tim until Tim was eventually
made interim CEO in 2009.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:53.599
Steve would come back later that year, but
in early 2011, he would take another medical

00:06:53.599 --> 00:06:58.330
leave of absence before he eventually resigned
in August of 2011 which made Tim Cook the

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:00.520
permanent CEO.

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:05.759
TIM’S LEGACY:
The first few years of Tim’s leadership

00:07:05.759 --> 00:07:09.410
were rather easy given that iPhone adoption
was still yet to peak.

00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:14.810
There were also plenty of new obvious features
to introduce like touch ID and a larger screen.

00:07:14.810 --> 00:07:20.130
But, iPhone sales would eventually not only
peak but start to decline, and this is when

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:23.560
Tim’s skills as a CEO would really be put
to the test.

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:27.290
Tim addressed this decline with a two fold
solution with the first part being making

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:29.569
Apple a lifestyle company.

00:07:29.569 --> 00:07:33.750
While Apple products have always been expensive,
it wasn’t till the last couple of years

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:37.639
that iPhones started getting a reputation
for being overpriced.

00:07:37.639 --> 00:07:41.560
Now don’t get me wrong here, people have
criticized the price of the iPhone since the

00:07:41.560 --> 00:07:46.450
day that it was launched, but I think the
price argument has shifted substantially.

00:07:46.450 --> 00:07:51.330
Initially, the argument had less to do with
the device itself being overpriced and more

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:54.900
to do with it being too expensive for the
average person to buy.

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:59.610
Today, the argument has shifted more towards
the device itself being overpriced.

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:04.400
But ironically, entry level iPhones are actually
cheaper than ever before.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:10.069
Apple keeps around older generations for longer
than ever before and the iPhone SE starts

00:08:10.069 --> 00:08:13.610
at $429 which is cheaper than the original
iPhone.

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:18.070
I think this is all part of Tim’s strategy
to maintain Apple’s exclusive image.

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:22.259
If Apple’s presentations focused on how
their entry level phones are cheaper than

00:08:22.259 --> 00:08:25.820
ever before, that would likely degrade Apple’s
image.

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:32.060
However, by focusing on the pro and pro max
versions that cost over $1000, Tim is brilliantly

00:08:32.060 --> 00:08:35.960
able to get the media to paint Apple’s products
as exclusive and desirable.

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:40.540
I believe other overpriced products like the
Apple wheels and the Pro stand serve the same

00:08:40.540 --> 00:08:41.540
purpose.

00:08:41.540 --> 00:08:45.580
These products simply define Apple’s image
while Apple’s real revenue drivers are their

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:47.050
cheaper products.

00:08:47.050 --> 00:08:51.720
Aside from crafting this genius balance between
brand image and practical products, Tim also

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:55.260
reduced Apple’s dependence on the iPhone
by embracing services.

00:08:55.260 --> 00:09:00.380
iCloud, Apple Music, and Apple TV have all
been massive hits for Apple.

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:05.780
As of October 2020, Apple literally had 585
million paid subscribers.

00:09:05.780 --> 00:09:10.100
Even if each of these subscribers only pay
Apple $5 per month, we’re talking about

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:15.250
nearly $3 billion in revenue per month and
$35 billion in revenue per year.

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:19.040
Considering this, it’s not surprising that
Apple’s revenue figures are higher than

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:22.780
ever before even though iPhone sales peaked
7 years ago.

00:09:22.780 --> 00:09:26.940
Aside from these services providing Apple
with a great source of revenue, these services

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:32.500
also strengthen the Apple ecosystem and make
it harder and harder for users to leave.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:37.110
And I think these three factors are what will
become Tim's legacy at Apple.

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:40.880
He defined Apple’s brand image, he significantly
reduced their dependence on iPhone sales,

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:43.360
and he crafted the Apple ecosystem.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:49.310
TIM’S FINAL PRODUCT:
Looking forward, it looks like Tim is excited

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:54.140
to release one more product which is likely
either the Apple Glasses or the Apple Car.

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:57.980
The glasses would be a major step into the
AR market while the Apple Car would be a major

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:01.070
step into the EV and autonomy industries.

00:10:01.070 --> 00:10:05.650
Either product could be a great sendoff product
for Tim, but if the final product is the car,

00:10:05.650 --> 00:10:08.970
then Tim will likely have to stick around
for a lot longer.

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:12.400
After all, scaling EV production is no easy
task.

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:17.350
And given that Tim has publicly announced
that he probably won't be CEO in 10 years,

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:21.530
I’m inclined to believe that the last product
is likely the AR glasses.

00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:24.770
Launching an EV vehicle would simply be a
much longer commitment.

00:10:24.770 --> 00:10:29.130
Also, the AR glasses are much more aligned
with Apple’s other products.

00:10:29.130 --> 00:10:34.130
A car is completely out of left field, and
Apple really has no prior experience in even

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:35.550
building a car.

00:10:35.550 --> 00:10:40.150
They’ve already experienced several delays
and hurdles along the way, and I wouldn’t

00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:42.870
be surprised if this project was scrapped
altogether.

00:10:42.870 --> 00:10:49.340
So, the most likely scenario is that we see
Apple Glasses roll out in 2025 or 2026 followed

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:51.940
by Tim Cook resigning near the end of the
decade.

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:57.100
In terms of a successor, the fan favorite
seems to be Craig Federighi which is not surprising

00:10:57.100 --> 00:10:59.670
given his charisma and stage presence.

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:03.860
But, experts feel that Craig is better suited
to continue leading software development as

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:05.200
opposed to becoming CEO.

00:11:05.200 --> 00:11:11.020
I gotta say though, if Craig did become CEO,
Apple’s presentations would be quite fun,

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:12.740
almost like Steve Job’s presentations.

00:11:12.740 --> 00:11:14.950
But, we’ll just have to wait and see.

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:20.970
In the meantime, Apple stock has already grown
10x under Tim’s leadership, so at this point,

00:11:20.970 --> 00:11:26.020
for Tim, it’s just a matter of wrapping
up his journey with a bow and saying farewell.

00:11:26.020 --> 00:11:29.440
When do you guys think Tim will retire and
who do you think will replace him?

